OK, after some minor difficulties, I have 3.4.2 working for tiles, 
and after no difficulties have the terminal version working too.

"What a pain they don't share bones/save/logfile/record", I thought 
to myself. It's also a nuisance that I prefer different .nethackrc 
settings depending on the version [I use number_pad in terminal, 
but not in Qt, for example]

A symbolic link later, and they almost do. The only sticking point 
is the terminal version uses compress and the tiles version uses 
gzip. [Thanks again Stanislav]

Because I'm not a unix buff, and because I'm the only user of my 
machine, I may be overlooking something, but it seems I should be 
able to automate the changing of compression format at launch time 
(of either version). I plan to try it using an applescript. Here's 
the outline of the idea:

Create 2 .nethackrc files (named with a .qt and a .term extension, 
for argument's sake). On launch of applescript user is asked 
whether they want to play in terminal or in Qt. Whichever is chosen 
the save/bones files that need to be converted are decompressed and 
recompressed, and the appropriate .nethackrc duplicated & renamed. 
And then either terminal launched/activated, or the tiles version 
started.

Is this a stupid idea (is there a better way)?
If not, and I get it working, anyone else interested in a copy?
[It will assume the location of .nethackrc, of where nethack is 
installed, and that it is in the same folder as the Qt executable. 
It will be saved as an editable application, so people can wonder 
that I have the audacity to call myself a programmer]

[I would like to see a NetHack for Mac that went to terminal or Qt 
depending on OPTIONS=windowtype:<foo> - but I guess that won't 
happen until I can be bothered trying for myself...]

